Below is a clear, step-by-step solution to complete your assignment exactly as asked.
I’ll use Jenkins master–agent (slave) architecture.

🔹 Common Concept (for all 3 cases)
Master → Jenkins controller (job scheduling, UI)
Slave/Agent → Executes builds
Communication → SSH (port 22)
Jenkins → Master installs jobs, agent runs jobs

✅ CASE 1: Ubuntu Master + Ubuntu Slave (Same network / VM)
Step 1️⃣ Create 2 Ubuntu machines
Ubuntu 20.04 / 22.04
Name them:
jenkins-master
jenkins-slave
Step 2️⃣ Install Jenkins on Ubuntu Master
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jdk
curl -fsSL https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2023.key | sudo tee \
/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc > /dev/null
echo deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] \
https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/ | sudo tee \
/etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y jenkins
sudo systemctl start jenkins
sudo systemctl enable jenkins

Access:

http://<master-ip>:8080

Password:

sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
Step 3️⃣ Prepare Ubuntu Slave
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jdk openssh-server
sudo systemctl enable ssh
sudo systemctl start ssh

Create Jenkins user:

sudo useradd -m jenkins
sudo passwd jenkins
Step 4️⃣ Setup SSH from Master → Slave

On master:

sudo su - jenkins
ssh-keygen
ssh-copy-id jenkins@<slave-ip>

Test:

ssh jenkins@<slave-ip>
Step 5️⃣ Add Slave in Jenkins UI
Jenkins → Manage Jenkins
Nodes
New Node
Name: ubuntu-slave
Type: Permanent Agent
Launch method: SSH
Host: <slave-ip>
Credentials: jenkins user
Save → Agent connects ✅
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ CASE 2: Ubuntu Master + CentOS Slave
Difference: Only slave OS changes
Step 1️⃣ Ubuntu Master

👉 Same as Case 1 (No change)

Step 2️⃣ Prepare CentOS Slave
sudo yum update -y
sudo yum install -y java-17-openjdk openssh-server
sudo systemctl enable sshd
sudo systemctl start sshd

Create Jenkins user:

sudo useradd jenkins
sudo passwd jenkins

Allow SSH:

sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ssh
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
Step 3️⃣ SSH setup & Jenkins UI

👉 Same steps as Case 1 (Step 4 & 5)

✅ Ubuntu master → CentOS slave connected
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ CASE 3: Ubuntu Master on GCP + Ubuntu Slave on AWS

This is cross-cloud Jenkins setup 🌍

Step 1️⃣ Create VM on GCP (Master)
Ubuntu VM
Allow ports:
22 (SSH)
8080 (Jenkins)

Install Jenkins → same as Case 1

Step 2️⃣ Create EC2 on AWS (Slave)
Ubuntu EC2
Security Group:
SSH (22) → GCP public IP

Install Java + SSH:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y openjdk-17-jdk openssh-server

Create Jenkins user:

sudo useradd -m jenkins
sudo passwd jenkins
Step 3️⃣ Network Connectivity

✔ GCP VM can SSH into AWS EC2
Test from GCP:

ssh jenkins@<aws-ec2-public-ip>
Step 4️⃣ Jenkins Node Configuration

Same Jenkins UI steps:

New Node
SSH
Host: AWS EC2 public IP
Credentials: Jenkins user

✅ Jenkins master on GCP → slave on AWS connected

📌 Final Verification (All Cases)

Run test job:

echo "Hello from Jenkins Slave"
hostname
java -version

You should see:

Job runs on slave
Correct OS shown
